<%# The three load the SAME document — one that already carries comment marks — and each
    writes into its own input. The only difference is `format:`. %>
<div class="space-y-10">
  <section data-test="adaptive">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">format: :json — the default, adaptive</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mb-2">
      With comment marks in the document it writes the ProseMirror shape so as not to lose
      them. That is right as far as the editor is concerned, and it is what changed the
      schema of the host's column without the host asking for it.
    </p>
    <%= render Bali::BlockEditor::Component.new(
          editable: true, format: :json, input_name: 'adaptive[content]',
          comments: comments, initial_content: content
        ) %>
  </section>

  <section data-test="pinned-blocks">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">format: :blocks — pinned to editor.document</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mb-2">
      The column stays an Array of blocks no matter what. Each thread's anchor is not
      persisted — the threads stay in their own store — and the editor warns on the console
      the first time it drops a mark.
    </p>
    <%= render Bali::BlockEditor::Component.new(
          editable: true, format: :blocks, input_name: 'pinned_blocks[content]',
          comments: comments, initial_content: content
        ) %>
  </section>

  <section data-test="pinned-prosemirror">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">format: :prosemirror — pinned to the ProseMirror shape</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mb-2">
      The other half of the decision: the column is always <code>{ type: "doc" }</code>,
      comments or no comments.
    </p>
    <%= render Bali::BlockEditor::Component.new(
          editable: true, format: :prosemirror, input_name: 'pinned_prosemirror[content]',
          comments: comments, initial_content: content
        ) %>
  </section>
</div>
